use /lib/UiComponent

from /components/buttons/Button import Button

export ui-component FloatingActionButton:
    description:
        A floating action button is a prominent button that floats above the
        content in a fixed spot, such as the bottom corner of the screen. It is
        for the one main action on that screen, and stays put while the content
        scrolls underneath it.

    composes:
        - @{Button}

    content:
        The same as any button: a label, an icon, or both.

    placement:
        It is fixed to the screen or view, not to the content, so it stays in
        the same spot and within reach while the content scrolls. It doesn't
        use Positioning, which is for popups attached to something.

    overlap:
        It never permanently hides anything. The view leaves enough room that
        the user can still reach whatever is underneath it.

    order:
        Its place in the Tab order, and the order a screen reader reads it in,
        depends on what it does in the view, not where it is drawn. The view
        decides where that is.

    nonGoals:
        - It doesn't open into several actions. That's a "speed dial", which is
            a separate component
        - It doesn't hide itself while scrolling. Whether it does is up to the
            library or the app
        - There's no hard rule of one per screen. That's advice on how to use
            it, not something the component enforces
